\f1\fs26 	Alexander\'92s father was an astute executive, but lacked the flare and drive of his wife. After the initial adjustment period (allocations never run smoothly at first), they actually formed a close bond, a partnership of trust and mutual respect that, for him, even extended to the metaphysical. Like everyone else he had had been taught at school that the economy was of paramount importance. The family unit was an historical oddity that had endured to provide a steady supply of labour for industry and the professions. The means of production had a veracious appetite and demanded fresh bodies, caring not who it consumed. \
Following the allocation, Shakti Senior had been very surprised by his genuine warm feelings for his wife. The birth of his only child Alexander had been a major source of happiness as a young married man. He knew he had genuinely loved his wife, but he could not tell her. What would she think of him? Love undermines rational thought. Profit demands rational thought. Love, therefore does not make economic sense and his wife needed a home based in economic rationality. Like a good husband, he never told his wife that he loved her, even when the words were bursting from his very core. As for his wife, she never gave love a single thought. She was very happy with her social position and would have reacted in a typically aggressive manner if anything, even her husband, threatened that position. He would not have survived. \
The home they created for Alex was the picture perfect environment for the budding young corporate raider. Every activity engaged in; every toy played with; every vid-stick watched and playgroup attended was training for the corporate sector. None of it sunk in. Well, that was not entirely true. He could recount the information that he had been taught but, seemingly, could not apply the theory to practical situations. To his parents he was a dullard and would only progress with a great deal of luck. \

